1
00:00:00,500 --> 00:00:04,047
Try this. Take one finger and tickle the bottom of your own

2
00:00:04,047 --> 00:00:04,343
foot.

3
00:00:04,723 --> 00:00:07,911
Nothing. Maybe a little itch. Honestly? Kind of boring.

4
00:00:08,361 --> 00:00:12,238
But if your friend did the exact same thing, same spot, same

5
00:00:12,238 --> 00:00:14,177
pressure, you'd be kicking and screaming.

6
00:00:14,677 --> 00:00:18,195
Same skin. Same touch. Completely different reaction.

7
00:00:18,575 --> 00:00:21,345
So what does your friend's finger have that yours doesn't?

8
00:00:22,145 --> 00:00:26,345
It's one of the strangest little glitches of the human body. Almost

9
00:00:26,345 --> 00:00:30,195
everyone can be tickled. And almost no one can tickle themselves.

10
00:00:30,645 --> 00:00:34,135
And the answer isn't in your skin. Or your foot. Or your

11
00:00:34,135 --> 00:00:34,426
fingers.

12
00:00:34,806 --> 00:00:38,447
It's in your brain. And it turns out your brain has been

13
00:00:38,447 --> 00:00:41,178
quietly editing your reality, every second of your life.

14
00:00:41,778 --> 00:00:43,645
So, why can't you tickle yourself?

15
00:00:44,745 --> 00:00:49,560
Scientists started poking at this question back in 1971. A team led

16
00:00:49,560 --> 00:00:54,375
by Lawrence Weiskrantz tickled people on the soles of their feet, and

17
00:00:54,375 --> 00:00:56,381
confirmed what we all suspected.

18
00:00:56,761 --> 00:00:59,196
It really is less ticklish when you do it yourself.

19
00:00:59,696 --> 00:01:05,038
Scientists even have two words for it. Knismesis is the light, feathery,

20
00:01:05,038 --> 00:01:10,381
itchy kind. Gargalesis is the heavy, poke-in-the-ribs kind that makes you howl

21
00:01:10,381 --> 00:01:11,271
with laughter.

22
00:01:11,651 --> 00:01:15,653
And it's that laughing kind that's nearly impossible to give yourself.

23
00:01:16,153 --> 00:01:21,209
But nobody could explain why. Not until 1998, when Sarah-Jayne Blakemore and

24
00:01:21,209 --> 00:01:25,844
her colleagues in London took the question inside a brain scanner.

25
00:01:26,224 --> 00:01:30,086
Volunteers lay in the scanner while a soft tickling device brushed across

26
00:01:30,086 --> 00:01:31,695
the palm of their hand.

27
00:01:32,075 --> 00:01:37,306
Sometimes the experimenter moved it. Sometimes the volunteers moved it themselves.

28
00:01:37,756 --> 00:01:40,994
When someone else did the tickling, the parts of the brain that

29
00:01:40,994 --> 00:01:42,073
process touch lit up.

30
00:01:42,454 --> 00:01:46,812
But when the volunteers tickled themselves, those same areas were much quieter.

31
00:01:47,262 --> 00:01:50,704
Same touch on the skin. But the brain was barely listening.

32
00:01:51,304 --> 00:01:55,447
And there was another clue. A region at the back of the

33
00:01:55,447 --> 00:01:58,899
brain, called the cerebellum, behaved differently when people tickled themselves.

34
00:01:59,279 --> 00:02:03,650
The cerebellum is famous for coordinating movement. It's the reason you can

35
00:02:03,650 --> 00:02:07,293
walk, type, and catch a ball without thinking about it.

36
00:02:07,673 --> 00:02:11,966
But the scientists suspected it was doing something else too. Something sneaky.

37
00:02:12,666 --> 00:02:14,187
It was predicting the future.

38
00:02:15,187 --> 00:02:19,335
Here's how it works. Every time you decide to move, your brain

39
00:02:19,335 --> 00:02:23,137
sends a command to your muscles. Lift. Move left. Press down.

40
00:02:23,517 --> 00:02:27,278
But it also keeps a copy of that command. Scientists call it

41
00:02:27,278 --> 00:02:31,039
an efference copy. Think of it like the carbon copy of an

42
00:02:31,039 --> 00:02:32,293
email you just sent.

43
00:02:32,673 --> 00:02:36,487
Your brain uses that copy to run a quick simulation. If I

44
00:02:36,487 --> 00:02:39,029
do this, here's exactly what I should feel.

45
00:02:39,479 --> 00:02:44,563
Then the real sensation arrives, and the brain compares the two. Prediction,

46
00:02:44,563 --> 00:02:45,410
versus reality.

47
00:02:45,910 --> 00:02:49,662
If they match, the brain basically says, that was just me, nothing

48
00:02:49,662 --> 00:02:52,476
to see here. And it turns the volume down.

49
00:02:52,856 --> 00:02:57,119
If they don't match, that's a surprise. And surprises get the brain's

50
00:02:57,119 --> 00:02:57,830
full attention.

51
00:02:58,330 --> 00:03:02,420
A tickle from someone else is pure surprise. You can't predict exactly

52
00:03:02,420 --> 00:03:06,169
where their fingers will go next, how hard, or how fast.

53
00:03:06,549 --> 00:03:10,544
But your own fingers? Your brain knows the plan before your hand

54
00:03:10,544 --> 00:03:13,207
even moves. There's no surprise left to feel.

55
00:03:13,907 --> 00:03:16,343
And the researchers proved it, with robots.

56
00:03:16,723 --> 00:03:20,748
Volunteers moved a robot arm with one hand, and a second robot

57
00:03:20,748 --> 00:03:24,772
brushed the palm of their other hand. Normally, this felt pretty dull.

58
00:03:25,152 --> 00:03:29,080
But then the scientists secretly added a delay. Just a tenth of

59
00:03:29,080 --> 00:03:30,716
a second. Then two tenths.

60
00:03:31,096 --> 00:03:34,616
And suddenly, the volunteers' own touch started feeling tickly.

61
00:03:35,116 --> 00:03:39,331
They also twisted the direction, so the robot moved a little differently

62
00:03:39,331 --> 00:03:43,546
from the hand. Same result. The bigger the mismatch, the more ticklish

63
00:03:43,546 --> 00:03:44,248
it felt.

64
00:03:44,748 --> 00:03:48,003
So it's not about who is touching you. It's about whether your

65
00:03:48,003 --> 00:03:49,088
brain saw it coming.

66
00:03:50,088 --> 00:03:54,363
Now here's where it gets weird. This prediction system isn't just for

67
00:03:54,363 --> 00:03:58,282
tickling. It's running all the time, on almost everything you do.

68
00:03:58,662 --> 00:04:02,531
Right now, your eyes are jumping around this screen several times a

69
00:04:02,531 --> 00:04:06,399
second. Every jump should make the whole world lurch. But it doesn't.

70
00:04:06,799 --> 00:04:10,591
Your brain predicts each eye movement, and cancels out the motion before

71
00:04:10,591 --> 00:04:11,855
you ever notice it.

72
00:04:12,235 --> 00:04:16,858
It's not just humans, either. A cricket's chirp is incredibly loud. Loud

73
00:04:16,858 --> 00:04:19,555
enough to overwhelm its own sensitive ears.

74
00:04:19,935 --> 00:04:23,894
So every time it chirps, a single nerve cell sends a signal

75
00:04:23,894 --> 00:04:27,523
that dials down its own hearing, perfectly timed to each chirp.

76
00:04:28,023 --> 00:04:31,665
Even your own voice gets this treatment. When you talk, your brain

77
00:04:31,665 --> 00:04:34,397
turns down its response to the sound of you.

78
00:04:34,997 --> 00:04:39,219
Which raises a fun question. Why are we ticklish in the first

79
00:04:39,219 --> 00:04:41,330
place? Honestly, nobody knows for sure.

80
00:04:41,780 --> 00:04:45,609
Some of the most ticklish spots, like your neck, your ribs, and

81
00:04:45,609 --> 00:04:49,437
the soles of your feet, are also some of the most vulnerable.

82
00:04:49,437 --> 00:04:53,266
So one idea is that tickling is an alarm, tuned to anything

83
00:04:53,266 --> 00:04:55,180
crawling or poking where it shouldn't.

84
00:04:55,560 --> 00:04:59,706
Another idea is that it's social. Tickling is one of the very

85
00:04:59,706 --> 00:05:01,779
first games parents play with babies.

86
00:05:02,159 --> 00:05:06,260
And it's not just us. When scientists tickle rats, they let out

87
00:05:06,260 --> 00:05:10,362
chirps too high-pitched for us to hear. Some researchers think it's a

88
00:05:10,362 --> 00:05:14,463
kind of laughter. The rats even chase the hand, asking for more.

89
00:05:14,963 --> 00:05:19,116
Alarm or game, a tickle only works when it comes from the

90
00:05:19,116 --> 00:05:23,269
outside world. Which is exactly why your brain filters out the inside.

91
00:05:23,969 --> 00:05:28,425
But all this cancelling has a hidden cost. And a 2003 experiment

92
00:05:28,425 --> 00:05:31,024
revealed it in a very relatable way.

93
00:05:31,524 --> 00:05:35,718
Researchers sat pairs of people across from each other. One person pressed

94
00:05:35,718 --> 00:05:39,913
down on the other's finger. Then the second person had to press

95
00:05:39,913 --> 00:05:42,010
back with exactly the same force.

96
00:05:42,390 --> 00:05:45,479
Back and forth they went. Everyone was trying to be fair.

97
00:05:45,879 --> 00:05:50,645
But the force kept climbing. On average, about thirty-eight percent harder, every

98
00:05:50,645 --> 00:05:51,439
single turn.

99
00:05:52,039 --> 00:05:55,979
Nobody was cheating. Your brain dampens the feeling of your own push,

100
00:05:55,979 --> 00:05:59,919
so it always feels weaker than it really is. To match what

101
00:05:59,919 --> 00:06:03,531
you felt, you push harder. And so does the other person.

102
00:06:03,911 --> 00:06:07,438
Which means the next time someone says, you hit me harder! They

103
00:06:07,438 --> 00:06:09,201
might actually be telling the truth.

104
00:06:10,201 --> 00:06:13,804
So what happens when this prediction system doesn't work the way it

105
00:06:13,804 --> 00:06:14,104
should?

106
00:06:14,604 --> 00:06:19,474
In 2000, Blakemore's team worked with people with schizophrenia who hear voices,

107
00:06:19,474 --> 00:06:23,938
or feel like their actions are being controlled by someone else.

108
00:06:24,318 --> 00:06:28,436
For these patients, their own touch didn't get turned down. It felt

109
00:06:28,436 --> 00:06:31,868
just as intense, and just as ticklish, as someone else's.

110
00:06:32,368 --> 00:06:36,440
One leading idea is that when the brain's that-was-me signal gets weaker,

111
00:06:36,440 --> 00:06:40,512
your own actions, and even your own inner voice, can start to

112
00:06:40,512 --> 00:06:42,887
feel like they're coming from somewhere else.

113
00:06:43,687 --> 00:06:46,375
And that reveals the real answer to our mystery.

114
00:06:46,975 --> 00:06:50,933
You can't tickle yourself because your brain is constantly sorting the world

115
00:06:50,933 --> 00:06:54,892
into two piles. Things I did. And things that happened to me.

116
00:06:55,272 --> 00:07:00,242
Everything you cause gets predicted, and quietly muted. Everything you didn't cause

117
00:07:00,242 --> 00:07:01,899
gets flagged as important.

118
00:07:02,279 --> 00:07:05,770
It's a big part of why you feel like you. A body

119
00:07:05,770 --> 00:07:09,260
that's yours. Actions that are yours. A voice in your head that's

120
00:07:09,260 --> 00:07:09,551
yours.

121
00:07:10,151 --> 00:07:14,261
Tickling is just the moment this hidden system shows itself. Because a

122
00:07:14,261 --> 00:07:16,658
tickle only works when it's a surprise.

123
00:07:17,108 --> 00:07:19,393
And you can never truly surprise yourself.

124
00:07:20,593 --> 00:07:24,236
So the next time someone tickles you and you can't stop laughing,

125
00:07:24,236 --> 00:07:27,879
that's not a weakness. That's your brain doing its job, catching the

126
00:07:27,879 --> 00:07:29,700
one thing it couldn't see coming.

127
00:07:30,300 --> 00:07:34,162
If this rewired your brain a little, subscribe to Randos in 60s

128
00:07:34,162 --> 00:07:38,023
for more weird stuff about being you. See you in the next

129
00:07:38,023 --> 00:07:38,345
one.
